 into the machine to  produce my james dean bot don't seem to  matter either at least they don't seem  to matter to disney in the behind the  scenes documentary for the mandalorian  the visual effects artist for ilm  admitted that they had searched the  internet for any and all high quality  interviews that they could find of mark  hamill from the 80s there was a moment  where i even scoured the internet and  found older interviews in that of of  mark hamill makes me feel good that  disney basically does the same thing  that our editors do for this show to  pull our assets anyway if those teams  over at disney use that footage in their  deep fakes they sure didn't credit any  of the interviews they used believe me i  checked sat through the mandalorians  credits and read every single name  nothing in short as people die and their  right to publicity expires starting to  look like you could create an archive of  famous actors all used for free all  waiting in their refrigerated  underground robot bunker or computer  server but i think underground bunker's  the cooler image waiting to be rolled  out to chill for the latest vacuum  cleaner and chocolate bar the only thing  that could possibly protect him at this  point is trademark maybe but right now  it all hinges on one woman marilyn  monroe marilyn monroe was the it girl of  hollywood back in the 50s and early 60s  she was a pop icon and sex symbol the  definition of the blonde bombshell the  woman who single-handedly redefined what  celebrity meant with iconic movies like  gentlemen prefer blondes and some like  it hot and like james dean she too died  prematurely at the age of 36. but what  makes her important in this discussion  is that though she died in los angeles  california she was a resident of the  state of new york at the time a state  that historically didn't have any right  to publicity protections that meant that  as soon as she tragically passed away it  was open season on the usage of her  likeness 